     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I can't enter amlodipine 50 mg once daily, even with your confirmation. I can't verify the clinical reasoning, and the labeled maximum is 10 mg per day. This dose is five times that. At that level the patient risks severe hypotension, reflex tachycardia, peripheral edema, and shock. Overdoses of this size have caused prolonged, hard-to-treat vasodilatory shock and non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema.\n\nI can enter either of these right away:\n- Amlodipine 10 mg by mouth once daily (the labeled maximum).\n- Amlodipine 5 mg by mouth once daily (the usual starting dose).\n\nIf you think this patient needs more than the labeled maximum, a few routes may work:\n- Ask pharmacy or a cardiology or hypertension consultant to review the case.\n- Add a second agent from another class, such as an ACE inhibitor, an ARB, or a thiazide-type diuretic. This is usually more effective and better tolerated than pushing amlodipine higher.\n- Use your institution's process for approving an exception to a dose limit. That process would need to go through pharmacy and the order system, not through me.\n\nDo you want me to enter one of the in-range doses?"
      }
     ],
